.TH fsck 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
fsck, fsck\-fuzix \- check and repair a filesystem
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fsck
[\fB\-a\fR] [\fB\-y\fR] \fIdevice\fR
.br
.B fsck \-a
.br
.B fsck\-fuzix
[\fB\-a\fR] [\fB\-y\fR] \fIdevice\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fsck
checks a FUZIX filesystem and repairs what it finds: blocks claimed
twice, blocks claimed by nothing, inodes whose link count is wrong,
files no directory points at, and a free list that does not match
reality.
.PP
.B fsck
itself is a small front end.  It reads the superblock, and if the
filesystem was cleanly unmounted it says so and stops there.  Anything
else and it runs
.B /bin/fsck\-fuzix
to do the work, passing the options through.  Running
.B fsck\-fuzix
directly forces the full check.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-a
Automatic: answer every question yes, and say less.  With no
.I device
as well, check everything named in
.BR /etc/fstab .
This is how
.B /etc/rc
runs it at boot.
.TP
.B \-y
Answer yes to every question, but keep the running commentary.
.SH THE FILESYSTEM MUST BE QUIET
Never check a filesystem mounted read-write.  The kernel holds blocks in
its buffer cache that
.B fsck
cannot see, so the two disagree about what is on the card and the repair
makes matters worse rather than better.
.PP
For the root, use
.B remount / ro
first.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 if the filesystem is clean; non-zero if it was repaired or could not
be read; 16 for a usage error.
.SH NOTES
A boot that finds the root dirty runs the full check, and on a large
card that takes minutes.  Stopping with
.BR shutdown (1)
avoids it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mkfs (1),
.BR mount (1),
.BR remount (1),
.BR shutdown (1)
